Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
The disciples came to Jesus at that time. And they asked him, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him, and set him in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "Truly, I tell you, unless you change and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever receives a little child like this in my name, receives me.
But what if someone misleads one of these little ones who believe in me? If he does, better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
It will be terrible for the world because of the things that drive people to sin! Things like this must come. But what an awful thing for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to go to hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Beware of despising one of these little ones. I tell you: their angels in heaven always look at the face of my Father who is in heaven.
> <.>
What do you think? Let's suppose a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes wandering off. Doesn't he leave the ninety-nine sheep on the hills? Doesn't he go after the lost one?
I don't believe that I will tell you what it is, is a fact. If he finds those sheep, he will be happier with them than for the sake of one of the ninety-nine who did not go astray.
The same goes for your Father in heaven. He doesn't want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone sins against you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep the matter between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have won him back.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take with you one or two more, so that by the mouth of two or three witnesses every fact may be confirmed. (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat an irreligious person or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. Imagine that two of you on earth agree about anything you ask for. It will be done for you by my Father who is in heaven.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them." The servant who knew no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
When the king began to do this, they brought before him a man who owed him millions of dollars.
The man was unable to pay his debts. His master issued an order. The man, his wife, his children, and all his possessions were to be sold to pay off his debts.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me some time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
The lady felt sorry for him. She forgave him for what he had been and set him free.
But that servant went out afterwards, and found one of the other servants, who owed him a few dollars. So he seized him, and began to strangle him, saying, "Give me what you owe me!"
The other server fell to his knees. 'Give me some time,' he pleaded. 'I'll pay you back.'
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. And he will be held there until he can repay what he owes.
The other servants saw what happened. It worried them a lot. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then the master summoned the head servant. And he said to him, "You are a wicked servant. I have forgiven you all that you owe me because you entreated me."
Should you not have had mercy on the other servant, just as I had mercy on you?
His master handed him over to the boys, angry. He will be punished until he repays all his debts.
Thus your father who is in heaven will treat each of you, if you do not forgive your brother from your hearts.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. And they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every cause?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that he who created them from the beginning 'made them male and female'? (Genesis 1:27)"
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. Therefore, man should not separate what God has put together.
They asked, "So why then did Moses command to give the man his wife a bill of divorce and send her away from him?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart. But it was not like that from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man can only divorce his wife if she has been unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone is able to accept the idea of celibacy. Only those who have been given the ability to live without marriage can accept that.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Others have been made that way by other people. And there are those who have chosen this path for themselves to serve the kingdom of heaven. Whoever can accept living this way, must do so." Bring the little children to Jesus
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them. For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
And Jesus placed his hands on them, and then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good deed must I do to have eternal life?"
Jesus said, "Why do you ask me about what is good? There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, keep the commandments."
The man asked, "Which ones?" Jesus answered, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness."
Honor your father and your mother. Love your neighbor as yourself.
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments. What else must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. Then you will have treasure in heaven. And come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "Truly I tell you, it is difficult for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.
I also tell you: It is difficult for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly amazed. And they said, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for man is possible with God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What then will be our reward?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true. When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left his home or family or field for my sake will receive a hundred times more. And they will also inherit eternal life.
But many of the first shall be last; and many of the last shall be first.
The story of the vineyard workers.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a landowner. He went out early to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard. And I will give you the just wage.'
So they left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
About five o'clock he came out and found others standing about. He asked them, 'Why do you stand here all day long and do no work?'
They answered, 'Because no one of us was hired.' He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager of the workers. He said to him, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Begin with those who were hired last. Then go to those who were hired first."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
When those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more pay. But each of them also received his usual day's wage.
When they received it, they started complaining about the employer.
They said, "These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour, and you paid them the same as you paid us. We did most of the work and were exposed to the sun's heat all day."
The landowner answered one of them, saying: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the wage of the usual working day?"
Take your money and go. I want to give the ones I recently hired the same wage I gave you.
Isn't it my right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel wronged because I gave generously to others?
Therefore, the last will be first, and the first will be last." Jesus speaks again of his coming death.
Jesus was on his way to Jerusalem. Along the road, he took the twelve disciples aside to speak to them.
He said, "We are going to Jerusalem," "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they hand him over to the non-Jews, so that they may mock him and scourge and crucify him, and on the third day he will rise from the dead!" A mother asks Jesus to grant her the knowledge
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus, and her sons came with her. She fell down at his feet and asked him for a blessing.
Jesus asked, "What do you want?" And she said, "Promise me that one of my two sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise that the other will sit at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am about to drink?" They said to him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right or left. These places are reserved for those my Father has prepared."
The other ten students heard about this. They became angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said to them, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Instead, everyone among you who wants to be great must be your servant.
And anyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Become like the Son of Man. For he did not come to be served, but to serve others and to give his life as a ransom for many."
Jesus and His disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed Him.
There was a blind man sitting by the roadside. Hearing that Jesus was passing by, he called out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me!"
The crowd demanded that they stop, and he ordered them to be quiet. But the two men cried out even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked them.
They answered, "Our lord, we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes. And they immediately saw. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And when they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethany, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He told them, "Go to the village ahead of you. When you arrive, you will find a donkey tied up, and its little donkey will be with it. Untie them both and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, tell them the Lord needs them. The Master will send them immediately."
This matter happened so that what was said on the tongue of the Prophet would be fulfilled. The text says,
Say to the daughter of Zion, "Behold, your king is coming to you, just and having salvation, lowly and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the daughter of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a load. And they put their coats on both of them. Then Jesus sat on the coats.
A large crowd gathered and threw their cloaks on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and some people followed him. Everyone shouted, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, saying, "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus cleansed the temple.
Jesus entered the temple of the Lord. And he began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers and the chairs of those who sold doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer."' (Isaiah 56:7) But you are making it 'a den of robbers.' " (Jeremiah 7:11)
He came to him in the temple, blind and lame people, and he healed them.
The chief priests and the scribes saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple courtyard shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
They asked Him, "Do you hear what these children are saying?" And Jesus answered them, "Yes, have you never read in the Scriptures: 'From the mouths of children and infants you have established praise'"? (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. And He remained there overnight. A fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He went up to it, but found nothing on it except leaves. He said to it, "Never shall fruit grow on you again!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. "How did the fig tree dry up so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus answered and said, "Assuredly, I say to you, you must have faith, and not doubt. For truly I say to you, if you have faith as a mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, 'Move hence to the sea,' and it will be done. Therefore I tell you, whatever things you ask when you pray, believe that you have received them, and you will have them."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer." A questioning of Jesus' authority.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. And they said to him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven or from men?" And they began to say to one another, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Then why did you not believe him?'
But what if we say, 'Men'? We fear people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "We do not know." And Jesus said to them, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these works." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
The son answered, "I will not," but then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, 'I will, sir.' But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father? They said, "The first." And Jesus said to them, "Truly I say to you, that tax collectors and sinners come into the kingdom of God before you."
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You saw that. And yet, you did not turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man had a piece of land, and he planted a vineyard in it. He surrounded it with a fence. He dug a pit for a winepress in it. And he also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he traveled far away on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. And they threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had done at first. The tenants treated the servants in the same way.
Finally, he sent them his son. And he said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the Son coming. They said to one another, "This is the Heir! Come, let's kill him and take his inheritance."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard comes back, what will he do to those tenants?
They answered: 'Those bad tenants will be destroyed, and then he will rent the vineyard to other tenants, who will give him his share at the proper time.'
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone. This was from the Lord, and it is amazing in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22-23)
This is what I am telling you. The dominion of God that is with you will be taken away from you and given to a people who produce its fruit.
Whoever falls on this stone will be shattered to pieces. But the stone itself will crush whoever falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard the stories of Jesus. And they knew that he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Dinner Party Story at the Wedding
Jesus told them more stories. And he said,
This is what the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent out other servants. He told them, "Tell those invited, 'I have prepared my dinner, and I have killed my calves and fattened cattle. All is ready.' Come to the wedding feast.'"
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The remaining ones took his servants. They treated them harshly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "Dinner is ready. But the guests were not fit to attend."
Go into the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered everyone they could find, good and bad. And in just moments, the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He found a man there who was not wearing the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he said to her, "how did you get in here without wearing the wedding clothes?" The man found nothing to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Bind his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will cry and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His words.
They sent their disciples to him. And they sent along with them the Herodians. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. You do not care about their status or importance."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus was aware of their evil schemes. He said, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes. Bring him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose are these words?"
They answered, "Caesar," and then He said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's. And render to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On that same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe in people rising from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses said, 'If a man dies and leaves no children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's family line.'"
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened with the second and third brothers. And it kept repeating until it reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? For all seven had her as a wife.
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will their fathers give them away in marriage; for they will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead who rise? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) For He is not a God of the dead, but of the living."
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The Great Commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law. So he tested Jesus with a question.
He said to her, "Teacher, which commandment is the most important in the law?"
Jesus answered, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets is based on these two commandments." From whom is Christ descended?
The Pharisees met together, and Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Who is he the son of?" They said, "He is the son of David."
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "my Lord," how could he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any more questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they command you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves are not willing to move even a finger to transfer them.
All they do is for the sake of others' seeing it. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing verses from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the fringes of their coats very long.
They love to sit in places of honor at banquets. They also love to take the first seats in assemblies.
They like to be called "rabbi" when greeting them in the markets.
But you should not be called 'Rabbi'. You have only one Master and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father'. You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called 'Teacher.' You have one Teacher, and that is Christ.
The most important person among you will be a servant to you.
He who exalts himself shall be abased; and he who abases himself shall be exalted.
Oh, what a terrible thing will happen to you, you nitpickers and Pharisees! You frauds! You lock the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You neither enter yourselves nor do you allow those who would enter to do so.
> <.>
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cross sea and land to make a single convert, and then you make them twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
Woe to you, blind guides! You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing; but if someone swears by the gold of the temple, he must keep his oath.'
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple that sanctifies the gold?
You also say, 'If anyone swears by the altar, there is nothing wrong; but if anyone swears by the offering on it, he is bound by his oath.'
O ye men of blind! Which is more important? Is it the gift? Or the altar that sanctifies the gift?
Every one that swears shall swear by the altar and by everything on it.
Whosoever shall swear by the temple of God, his oath is by God, and by Him who dwelleth therein.
And everyone who swears by the name of heaven thereby swears by the name of God's throne and by the one who sits upon it.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe in the dill and cumin and neglect the weightier matters of the law: justice and mercy and faith. These you ought to have done, without leaving the others undone.
O ye blind guides! Ye make clean the outside of the cup and of the platter; but within, ye are full of extortion and excess.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of decay and death on the inside. You also outwardly appear righteous to people, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
You blind Pharisee! Cleanse first what is within the cup and the dish; then the outside of the cup and the dish will be clean also.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside, but inside they are full of the bones of the dead and everything unclean.
The matter with you is the same. From the outside, you appear to be doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are filled with what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! You build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous.
She says, 'If we had lived in the days of those who lived before us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets.'
You bear witness against yourselves. You confess that you are the descendants of a people who killed the prophets.
Do you complete a deficiency in those who were before you?
O den of vipers! How will you escape the sentence of the fire?
Therefore, I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify others, and you will flog others in your synagogues, and you will persecute them from city to city.
Therefore, you will pay the price in blood for all the righteous ones that have been killed on earth. I mean, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berekiah. You have killed him between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is really true. All this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. I have often wanted to gather your people together, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples caught up with him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
Jesus asked, "Do you see all these things? Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. And they said to him, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Take care that no one deceives you.
Many will come in my name, saying, 'I am he, the Messiah!', and they will mislead many.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not be alarmed. Such things must happen. But the end is not yet. You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not be alarmed. Such things must happen. But the end is not yet.
Nation will fight against nation. Kingdom will fight against kingdom. People will be hungry. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All of these are the beginning of labor pains.
Then people will deliver you to tribulation, and they will kill you, and you will be hated by all nations for my name's sake.
In those days, many will fall away from the faith and will hate one another. And many false prophets will arise and lead many astray. And because lawlessness will be increased, the love of many will grow cold. But the one who endures to the end will be saved. And this gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a witness to all the nations. Then the end will come.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But the one who stands firm to the end will be saved.
This good news about the Kingdom will be preached in the whole world as a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the abomination that maketh desolate." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) You will one day see him standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand.
Then those in Judea must flee to the mountains.
No one on the roof should go down to their home to get anything.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
How dreadful those days will be for pregnant women! How dreadful they will be for nursing mothers!
Hopefully, you won't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be a terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else from the beginning of the world until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If the days were not shortened, no one would survive. But for the sake of God's chosen people, they will be shortened.
At that time, someone might tell you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "He is over there!" Do not believe it.
False messiahs and false prophets will appear. They will perform great signs and wonders. And they will seek to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go to him. Or if someone says, "He is deep in the house," do not believe it.
Lightning, seen from the east, will be visible in the west; and so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever a carcass is found.
After the terrible ordeal of that day, "the sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood. And the stars of heaven shall fall, and the powers that are in heaven shall be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with a great trumpet sound. They will gather the chosen ones from all four directions. They will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. When its branches become soft and its leaves sprout, learn that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the doorstep.
What I am about to tell you is true. The people living at that time will not die until all those things have happened.
The heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knoweth the day or the hour, but my Father only.
No one knows about that day or that hour. Even the angels in heaven do not know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how the days were in the time of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They did all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea what was going to happen until the flood came and took them all. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Six mills, two grinding on a manual millstone, the other four not grinding. One of the two grinding on the manual millstone will be taken, and the other will be left.
Therefore, be on your guard; you do not know on what day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Let's suppose the homeowner knew when the thief would come at night. Then he would make sure to keep watch. He would not allow his home to be broken into.
Therefore, you also must be ready. For the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
A master is to place one of his servants over the rest of the servants in his house. The servant's task is to feed them at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant for this task.
It will be good for the servant if his master finds him doing his work when he returns.
What I am about to tell you is real. The master will make that servant responsible for everything he owns.
But suppose that this slave is wicked; suppose that he says to himself, 'My master is away so long.'
He is supposed to start hitting the other servants. And he is supposed to eat and drink with those who drink heavily.
The master of that servant will return on a day the servant does not expect. And he will return in an hour the servant does not know.
Then the master will chop him into pieces. He will send him to the place where the prosecutors go. There people will weep and gnash their teeth.
The Parable of the Ten Virgins.
This is what the kingdom of heaven will be like at that time. Ten virgins went out to take their lamps to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. And five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they did not take any olive oil with them.
The wise men carried oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. Therefore, all the bridesmaids felt tired and sleepy.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the bridesmaids woke up and got ready with their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, 'Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out.'
"No," they replied. "There may not be enough for everyone. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy for yourselves."
So they went to buy the oil. But on their way, the groom arrived. With him entered the bride's maids who were ready for the wedding dinner. Then the door was closed.
Later on, the other bridesmaids came too. "Sir! Sir!" they said. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, saying, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Stay awake. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The Parable of the Three Servants.
Again, this is what the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A man was going on a journey, so he called his servants and entrusted his property to them.
The man gave $10,000 to one. And he gave $4,000 to another. Then he gave $2,000 to the third. He had given each servant the amount he knew he was able to take care of. Then he went on his journey.
The server who received the ten thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money. He earned an additional ten thousand dollars.
The four thousand dollar owner received an additional four thousand dollars.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. And he buried his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money they had earned.
The man who had received ten thousand dollars came with the other ten thousand dollars. He said, "Sir, you entrusted me with ten thousand dollars. Look, I have won another ten thousand dollars."
His master answered and said, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You were faithful over a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
The man who had four thousand dinars also came. And he said, "My master, you have entrusted me with four thousand dinars, and now I have earned another four thousand dinars."
His master answered and said, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You were faithful over a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
Then the man to whom he had paid two thousand dollars came. And he said, "Sir, I knew that you were a hard man. You reap where you did not sow, and gather where you did not scatter."
So I was scared. I went out and buried your $2,000 in the ground. See, this is what's yours.
His master replied, "You wicked, lazy slave, did you know that I sow where I did not plant, and harvest where I did not scatter?
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have gotten it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants, saying, "Take the two thousand from him, and give them to him who has the twenty thousand."
To those who have, more will be given. They will have more than enough. But what about those who have nothing? Even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Cast out this contemptible servant. Out there in the darkness, people will weep and gnash their teeth.' The sheep and the camels.
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory, and all the angels with him. He will sit on his throne, and all the nations will be gathered before him.
All nations will be gathered before him. He will divide the people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd separating the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will speak to those on His right. He will say, "My Father has blessed you. Come and receive what is yours. It is the kingdom that was prepared for you from the creation of the world."
I was hungry, and you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty, and you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger, and you invited me into your home.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous will answer him, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you something to drink?'
When did we see you a stranger and take you in? And when did we see you needing clothes and clothe you?
When have we seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
The king will answer, saying, 'The very thing I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of these least brothers of mine, you did for me.'
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Cursed are you! Go away from Me into the eternal fire, prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not take me into your home. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and a prisoner. But you did not care for me.
They will also answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you something to drink? And when did we see you as a stranger and take you in, or naked and clothe you? And when did we see you sick or in prison and come to you?'
He will reply, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did not do for one of these little ones, you did not do for me."
Then they will go away to be punished forever. As for those who did right, they will receive eternal life.
The plot to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples,
As you know, Easter is two days away. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
The chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They met in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They planned to cleverly arrest Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "It may stir up the people." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster flask of very costly perfume, pure and unspotted. She broke the alabaster flask and poured it on His head.
When the students saw that, they were angry. "What is this wastefulness?" they asked.
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus was aware of this. He said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
There will always be poor people among you. But I will not always be with you.
I poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What you have done will be told in every place where the good news is preached to all the world. It will be told as part of her story." Judas agreed to hand Jesus over.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He said to them, "What will you give me if I deliver Jesus to you?" And they counted out to him thirty pieces of silver.
From that time on, Judas watched for an opportune time to deliver Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover meal?"
He answered them, "Go into the city to a certain man. Tell him, 'The Teacher says: My time is at hand to keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was at the table with the twelve.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were very sorrowful. And each of them began to say to Him, "It is not I, Lord, is it?"
Jesus answered, "He who dipped his hand with me in the dish will betray me."
The son of man will go away just as it is written about him. But woe to that one through whom the son of man is handed over! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born.
It was Judas who would betray him. He said, "Ain't it I, Rabbi?" Jesus answered, "Thou hast said."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. He gave thanks, broke it and gave it to his disciples. "Take, eat," he said. "This is my body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked and offered it to them. And he said, "Drink from it, all of you."
This is my blood, the blood of the new covenant. It is poured out for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
This is what I tell you. From now on, I will never again drink wine with you until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom."
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "Tonight, before the rooster crows, you will all disown me three times." It is written, "The Lord said to me, 'I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.' " (Zechariah 13:7)
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter answered, "Everyone might leave because of you. But I will never do so."
Jesus answered and said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. It will happen tonight, and before the rooster crows, you will deny knowing me three times."
Peter said, "I may die with you, but I will never say that I do not know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' prayer in Gethsemani.
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. And he said to them, "Sit here while I go there to pray."
He took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee. And he began to be sorrowful and very heavy.
Then He said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel the closeness of death. Stay here. Watch with Me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but thine, be done."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Are you not able to watch with me for one hour?"
Pay attention, pray. This way, you will not fall into sin when tempted. The spirit is willing. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out again. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as you will."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. And for the third time he prayed for the same thing.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look! The hour is near. The Son of Man is about to be delivered to the sinners."
Arise! Let us go! Here he is, the one who will deliver me to them!" The arrest of Jesus.
And as Jesus was speaking, Judas came, being one of the twelve. And a great multitude with him, having swords and staves, from the chief priests and the scribes.
Judas, who was going to betray Jesus, had already agreed upon a signal with them. "The one I kiss is the man," he had said. "Seize him."
So Judas immediately went to Jesus. He said to him, "Greetings, Teacher!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place, for all those who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will immediately send an army of angels, their number exceeding 70,000 angels.
But how do the written prophecies come true then? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus addressed the crowd, saying, "Am I a bandit, leading an armed gang, that I should have to fight you?" And he asked, "Should you not have to come out with swords and clubs to arrest me? For I was daily with you in the temple teaching, and you did not arrest me."
But all this has happened so that the words of the prophets may be fulfilled." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus was brought before the Sanhedrin.
Those who seized Jesus brought him to Caiaphas, the high priest. And there the teachers of the law and the elders had assembled.
He didn't go far, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and all the Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two other witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man said, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God and to rebuild it in three days.' "
Then the high priest stood up and said to Jesus, "Do you have no answer? What is your defense against these charges for which these men bear witness against you?"
Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us: are you the Christ, the Son of God?"
Jesus said, "Yes. Just as you have said." Then he added, "But let me tell you all. In the days ahead, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes and said, "He has spoken blasphemous words against God! Why do we need any more witnesses? You have all heard his evil blasphemy."
What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They punched him with their hands. Others slapped him.
They said, "Prophesy to us, Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the outer court. A servant girl came to him. And she said to him, "You also were with Jesus of Galilee."
But out of all of them, Peter said that he wasn't. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. And there another woman saw him. And she said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said again that it wasn't him. And he swore, "I don't know that man!"
Soon those who had been standing there came up to Peter and said to him, "You are surely one of them, for when you speak, a Galilean accent is discerned."
Then Peter began to curse himself and he swore to them, "I do not know the man!" And immediately the cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him: "The cock will not crow," Jesus had told him, "until you have denied knowing me three times." Peter went out and wept bitterly, overcome with weakness.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the high priests and elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, who was the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. He felt deep remorse and sorrow for what he had done. So he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I made a mistake, I handed over an innocent man." They answered him: "What do we care? This is your problem."
Judah threw the money into the temple and left. Then he went and hanged himself.
The senior priests picked up the coins. And they said, "It's against the law to put this money in the temple treasury. It's blood money. You've paid for the death of a man."
They decided to use the money to buy a pottery field. People from other countries will be buried in it.
That is why to this day it is called "The Field of Blood".
Then the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which they valued him from the sons of Israel."
"They used these coins to buy the potter's field, just as the Lord had instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9)
Jesus was standing before the governor. And the governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes. As you say."
But when the chief priests and elders accused him, he made no answer.
Pilate asked him, saying, "Do you not hear how many testimonies they give against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word, not even to a single charge. The governor was really astounded.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
At that time, they had a famous prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release? Barabbas? Or Jesus called the Christ?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He realized that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. It says: "You have nothing to do with this man. He is innocent. I have suffered greatly in a dream today because of him."
But the high priests and elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to demand the death of Jesus.
The governor asked, "Which one do you want me to release?" And they said, "Barabbas."
Pilate asked, "Then what shall I do with Jesus called the Christ?" And all the people answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done wrong?" Pilate asked. But they shouted even louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was making no progress. Instead, the crowd began to grow angry. So he took water and washed his hands before them. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he declared. "You are the ones responsible!"
All the people answered, "We and our children will bear the guilt of his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas. But he flogged Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's troops took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. And around him all the other soldiers gathered.
They stripped him and dressed him in a purple robe.
Then they twisted thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. And they put a stick in his right hand. Then they knelt down in front of him and mocked him. "Hail, King of the Jews!" they said.
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After they mocked him, they took the robe off him. They put his own clothes on him. Then they led him out to crucify him on the cross. Jesus was crucified on the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and offered it to Jesus to drink. And after tasting it, He refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his clothes among themselves by casting lots.
They sat there and guarded him.
And they put over His head His accusation written: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two robbers were crucified with him, one on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by Jesus, reviled Him and mocked Him. They shook their heads as they passed.
And they said, "You are destroying the temple and rebuilding it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
He was also ridiculed by the chief priests, the teachers of the law, and the elders.
They said, "He saved others," "but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if he wants him. It is he who said: "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus died.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who were standing there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately, someone ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. Then he offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the curtain of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. The earth shook. Rocks were split.
The tombs were opened. And many of the saints who had died, arose, who had been previously dead, and had repented of their sins.
They came out of the tombs. And after Jesus had raised them from the dead, they went into the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and the guards who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened. And they were filled with awe and said, "Certainly, he was the Son of God!"
It wasn't very far, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to meet his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. There was also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was there also. Jesus was buried.
In the evening, a rich man came from the city of Arimathea. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered it to be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed it in his new grave which he had dug in the rock. Then he put a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. After that, he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day, after the preparations, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Lord, we remember something that liar said when he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Therefore, give orders to secure the tomb until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body. Then they will tell the people that Jesus has risen from the dead. And this final lie will be worse than the first.
Pilate answered, "Take some guards with you. Go. Make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and secured the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone and posted guards to keep watch.
Jesus rose from the dead.
Now Saturday has ended. Dawn had broken on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb.
A strong earthquake occurred. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He moved the stone and sat on it.
His body shone like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like dead men.
The angel said to them, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are seeking Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, 'He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see him.' I have told you now.
The women hurried away from the tomb, running with joy. Although they were afraid, they were filled with happiness. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly Jesus met them. And he said, "Peace be with you!" They came to him, and they took hold of his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards' report to the chief priests.
While the women were going, some guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They said to the soldiers, "You have to say, 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.' "
If the Governor hears this report, we will bribe him. This will save you from trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story spread among the Jews to this day. The Final Instructions of Jesus to His Disciples
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him. But some still had their doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore, go and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And be sure that I am with you always, to the end of the age."
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, saying, "I will send my messenger before you. He will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight in the desert a highway for our God."
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him. And all the people from Jerusalem went out. And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John was proclaiming: "One is coming after me who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
"I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit."
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John the Baptist baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment, he saw the heavens split open. And he saw the Holy Spirit descend on him like a dove.
A voice called to him from heaven, saying, "You are my beloved Son; with you I am well pleased."
Immediately, the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. There the devil tempted him. Wild beasts did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose his first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee. He preached the good news of God.
He said, "The time has come," "for the Kingdom of God is near. Repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come. Follow me." "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets immediately and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further. There he saw James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in the boat mending their nets.
And He called them at once, and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and went after Him. And He cast out a demon that was mute, from a man.
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
The people were amazed at his teaching. He taught them as one who had authority, not like the teachers of the law.
At that moment, a man who was in their community, and was controlled by an evil spirit, cried out and said,
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One, the Holy God!"
Jesus said firmly, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a cry.
Everyone was amazed and started asking each other, "What is this? A new teaching! And with such authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
News about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those with him left the synagogue. Immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her. He took her hand and helped her to rise. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from various diseases. He also cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the towns nearby. I must preach there also. For this I have come."
Jesus went throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the good news of the kingdom. He also healed every disease and every ailment among the people. His fame spread throughout Syria, and all the sick brought to him those who were afflicted with various diseases and pains, demoniacs, epileptics, and paralytics. And he healed them.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before him and begged him, saying, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you can."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away and sternly warned the man.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this. Go and present yourself to the priest. Offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. And he spread the news to everyone. So Jesus could no longer enter a town openly. He remained outside in lonely places. But people kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had returned to his house.
So many people gathered that there was no room left, not even outside the door. And Jesus taught the word to them.
Four of the newcomers were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made a hole in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his mat through the hole.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there, reasoning within themselves,
Why is this man talking this way? He is saying something extremely evil! Only God forgives sins!
Immediately, Jesus realized what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?"
Is it easier for me to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, pick up your mat, and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He told her, "I'm telling you, get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man got up and took his mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching him. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus chose Levi
Again, Jesus went to the side of the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. And he began to teach them.
As he walked, he saw Levi son of Guni sitting at the tax collection site. And he said to him, "Follow me." And he got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the home of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
Some of the teachers of the law were Pharisees who were there. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. They said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said to them, "Those who are healthy do not need a physician, but those who are sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners."
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. And people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees, but your disciples do not fast. Why?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will fast.
People do not sew a patch of new cloth on an old garment. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old one. This would make the tear even worse.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the wineskins will be ruined. No, people put new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through fields of grain. The disciples began to pluck the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, it is not lawful to do this work on the Sabbath, why then do your disciples do it?"
He answered, saying, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the bread of presentation. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. And David also gave some of it to his men as well.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath."
So, the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day."
Healing on Saturday
One time, Jesus went into the synagogue. And there was a man who had a withered and crooked hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak and twisted hand. And he said to him, "Rise and stand before everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what must be done on the Sabbath? Is it lawful to do good or to do evil? Is it to bring life or death?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked around angrily. He was so angry because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was completely sound.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A great crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about all the things Jesus was doing. And many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea. From beyond the Jordan, and from the regions of Tyre and Sidon.
Jesus instructed his disciples, because of the crowd, to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from pressing around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing to touch him.
When the people who had evil spirits saw him, they fell down in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell anyone what this meant. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up on a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And they were with him from that time on. And he also sent them out to preach.
They had the power to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus surnamed them Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was also Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there was also Thaddaeus, and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was also one of them. He was the one who would later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again, crowds gathered. They were so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. So they went to take control. And they said, "He has lost his mind."
Some of the scribes were there. They had come from Jerusalem. And they said, "He is empowered by the prince of demons; that is why he drives out demons."
Jesus called his disciples and told them stories. He said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not endure.
If the family breaks apart, it will not survive.
